#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078360 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078510 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c2387a4-202d-528a-b0ca-3f87d5a06ea3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Order of Transformations Given a Transformed Function Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "bbd2751e-73b2-500b-abef-c67564a9559f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7622" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2-3\\right)+5$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}1)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}2)$ - Translate up by $5$ units\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}3)$ - Translate right by $6$ units\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 12$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhat is a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac x2-3\\right)+5 = f\\left(\\dfrac 12(x-6)\\right)+5\n$$\n\nTo get $y=f\\left(\\dfrac 12(x-6)\\right)+5,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}1)\\mathbin{:}$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac x2 \\right).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}3)\\mathbin{:}$ Translate $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2 \\right)$ right by $6$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}2)\\mathbin{:}$ Translate $y=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)$ up by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$$\n(\\textrm{T}1) \\to (\\textrm{T}3) \\to (\\textrm{T}2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078361 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17339421-a1ad-554f-aed8-28e20cebab10" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Three Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "c7dbe419-31b2-5c5a-bcab-738aa6fd46fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-7623" :question/problem "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Plot the graph of $y=f\\left(2x+2\\right)-1.$\n\n![](images/69/69e2a7112819db4536ea902c236c7d272fb8fb5ada1d114a59dcea5de212d4d5.png)" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny = f\\left(2x+2\\right) -1 = f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x).$\n\n![](images/b9/b9f7d5c0b8014bc3e37171efb5bcc6422573cb4dc3a3a3ed783ca10385fd9a86.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right).$\n\n![](images/d4/d4e0469b84d60010a5cd78ee9b575aec441180b615a2db5587099d65e4bf3496.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right)-1.$\n\n![](images/c6/c6ebcaa8fd8d421de53231e381130967ec35a270b35786d17a0e4c9e98420a9e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078362 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078512 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d69a3ef2-0854-503b-8586-b0113966d9ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Four Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "6ee9bee5-8027-5ba5-ad2c-9b697f2affc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7624" :question/problem "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Plot the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)+5.$\n\n![](images/b3/b314de8320a82ad5a7bd395da63c6f9bd4348ee2f4fd12c13a090c20fbcdd322.png)" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f\\left({3}(x+3)\\right)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left({3}x\\right).$\n\n![](images/92/92ffae3729220d6eae6cfb96fde2efc93f19b58ad38a5ba36760fb8b38ee8ced.png)\n\n- Then, translate it left by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left({3}(x+3)\\right).$\n\n![](images/c4/c4fc767f318d276a0178f4ceb2d2550a5141744ca2c3ed8da1f240309d3a1ca0.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right).$\n\n![](images/14/143a49c724c30a9e9cf432960fcf7046784a9a881182959875b9805adb0a8c5e.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it up by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)+5.$\n\n![](images/6d/6d7295138c2821d0d3c479ff9d98e2018bc26493ff09f41fe463cc31cee0907e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17339421-a1ad-554f-aed8-28e20cebab10" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Three Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2d9562-166b-55eb-a916-c3f87615193a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b7/b711b4f44379ecdee008c49c2ca3d8e5db703cd1ac8226958a6ed8dc6ff402e3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525233} {:answer/id #uuid "1978c38d-b7de-539c-990b-b3f16b07042a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/47df4de5e50212ab2e3eaad83a4e23e3abd9b907f8f8cb3c61b0b8c627cd8b6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525257} {:answer/id #uuid "27142361-4ff5-58fc-ac1e-99d2e6815a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20c8edc5f20f2a7f4aced4a16df28961e5ffc18ef6ddfe08cef96cce4bf53fa7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525299} {:answer/id #uuid "62fdffe8-c847-50a7-8c8e-f25cfe514f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f541aac8ff04f5ae19d526a648a9611d6676364fced3334811043284a173069f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525456} {:answer/id #uuid "ada6ecd6-651e-5d3b-b5e1-b4d9133cf235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c65aaa19cd151c307a65bfb68e293dfe8aa48da13d5ac327fbc89a774452177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ada6ecd6-651e-5d3b-b5e1-b4d9133cf235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c65aaa19cd151c307a65bfb68e293dfe8aa48da13d5ac327fbc89a774452177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525646} :answer-field/id #uuid "25d65630-7191-5a9a-a741-1d4ba90eb521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "721a221e-699a-5602-87be-07d47c9a3e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-113470" :question/problem "![](images/31/311dbd0e9cd00f766e9b83cff03c770e7c401ac576b50f43c889f5442f4a3436.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(x-1)-4?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(x-1)-4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- translate it to the right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$\n\n![](images/99/99629999257c08d4524c865b091111e1a5f13e02384bdec8b904612063fe8269.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(x-1).$\n\n![](images/16/1628b24115b5a674353600ca2e09eff3e2a9c9b8f963811577be5b9d56065ccb.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=2f(x-1)-4.$\n\n![](images/65/650e8fb922740060821e21a2f64ff28f1a6990c5187dba4c355ff0c28df1d5d0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17339421-a1ad-554f-aed8-28e20cebab10" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Three Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245f2eb4-6755-51f9-82ce-adb6c8c00485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/3820aedb3d0dbeeab0496b7db09084c63509edbef5bcb14f016051bcf6800288.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525294} {:answer/id #uuid "28ffcc66-6daf-5250-a9ad-4a8bc3bb76a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6a80453ec3e35f1e50db8790547012f78d202f96e478214590c43ac1b28d0188.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525304} {:answer/id #uuid "51891f0d-e9bc-5799-a234-4e3c08259d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/28019e2b244e976567b36e80bd396b604d4e436c38a06da3a5621d77236fb113.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525418} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c148a2-f9f6-59d8-bad0-09c845a1fc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/916f7bfc94115cb568945f035a314062bfafd4f244c581e7b9b7655002241a90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525632} {:answer/id #uuid "ee63fb98-113f-57ad-9c17-6c32e10df47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f1a136148ad5d8bf322cbdf0cd0eeab4919dfbeb1680064366c4dbd3c5d6a05e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8c148a2-f9f6-59d8-bad0-09c845a1fc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/916f7bfc94115cb568945f035a314062bfafd4f244c581e7b9b7655002241a90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525632} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e39b274-e03a-5bb7-b185-b8e7f2e4287f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4dd5481f-091a-50a2-889d-4cab50cc426d" :question/math-academy-id "q-87413" :question/problem "![](images/34/34f52b01b954b7ca3a829b43995aaf56a1def9f79b68028c55993c57b386eb30.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(4x+8)?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=2f(4x+8)=2f(4(x+2))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=2f(4(x+2)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(4x).$\n\n![](images/80/80978c68384f9837295ad48a8ea44beff351116c4bb1b4a6a79ecfe6f40136f4.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the left by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f(4(x+2)).$\n\n![](images/eb/eb1cf555fdbd31d88d695a166ef646396bbd38a160f23331c7775bfeb1bc3820.png)\n\n- Finally, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(4(x+2)).$\n\n![](images/89/89cd8b7a309924a5d7a4ca65539059521d07cf619f5751c400622094ba6e69e4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078512 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d69a3ef2-0854-503b-8586-b0113966d9ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Four Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055775d2-7fd3-50eb-9230-67b0fc5a747b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f3/f3595ce54cbe2d4aca680041411a4c50660500872316ddcc42bfffcd331c146f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525218} {:answer/id #uuid "212e684a-b560-5ddc-b66f-3ad6c40f2c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f0f6a7ad80414e94449720bf20002ec15db2dd4a2be5ed6d7f3a7539f44a5b32.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525281} {:answer/id #uuid "69bebd39-0ae6-537c-af79-f261f005cc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d37eb91b7b38f6b2835143f92809d736680ade4ac412c65d857e754d2acc7bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525475} {:answer/id #uuid "8150ddc7-9640-5cdf-bd03-3906e72e93c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/be/be3a906eb96b15dc74834349fed17c871e318e394b957bc7b3ddd21e54ae97e2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525531} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e9672f-da43-510c-a655-02c7da12b03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/011808bc67eb52a3d40613db08dc34609d564224932919b77fcdee9f3de7a2e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69bebd39-0ae6-537c-af79-f261f005cc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d37eb91b7b38f6b2835143f92809d736680ade4ac412c65d857e754d2acc7bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525475} :answer-field/id #uuid "70fbafa4-c6b5-5ea5-9fe0-c4f951b14d3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78476838-2c24-583d-9617-bd1c106f54aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-87483" :question/problem "![](images/60/601301adb254953bec91e126b2ff377864211ba047c741148e005c11682954c2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}x-1)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}x-1)+2=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3))+2\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3))+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$\n\n![](images/b8/b85eed09d973abb632eb92a3a9f2abb7a2c9aa09061778d9df22eac5d59ee86e.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)).$\n\n![](images/d0/d03aa20f465906c8cbcc65a1f42b208d7f0071732d47598158fc1c81ee6222ef.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)).$\n\n![](images/d2/d2ccc316205b537c9a457b81b9fb3ba88e63602bd0aaebc90f7cedfef05863cd.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3))+2.$\n\n![](images/b3/b3e358c3c3d2371b5c0af932857bf34065a079d223a0d1fdfcbd09cab32a8506.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078510 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c2387a4-202d-528a-b0ca-3f87d5a06ea3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Order of Transformations Given a Transformed Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2912b310-dcfc-58d9-a301-f74dc21ca34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525305} {:answer/id #uuid "5d1fc3fe-4d3a-5601-833d-d516345e3833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}5)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525445} {:answer/id #uuid "63c838e6-3ae8-51f3-bfeb-05ee5e412eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525460} {:answer/id #uuid "7dbbf3a4-a459-57f9-b3c5-72844ef15fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525517} {:answer/id #uuid "998bdf62-3464-5f81-93a2-0170d4c52613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63c838e6-3ae8-51f3-bfeb-05ee5e412eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525460} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a61aa8e-99e2-5211-ae4e-dfb981050f40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48d2170e-c4ab-503f-ae82-46d7eebc72a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-87484" :question/problem "The graph of $y=2f(3x+6)+1,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translate up by $1$ unit\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translate left by $2$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}5)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=2f(3x+6)+1=2f(3(x+2))+1\n$$\n\nTo get $y=2f(3(x+2))+1,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(3x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Translate $y=f(3x)$ left by $2$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(3(x+2)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Stretch $y=f(3(x+2))$ by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=2f(3(x+2)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Translate $y=2f(3(x+2))$ up by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=2f(3(x+2))+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078510 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c2387a4-202d-528a-b0ca-3f87d5a06ea3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Order of Transformations Given a Transformed Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f067d31-f7ed-5c95-8101-5e3457388941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525315} {:answer/id #uuid "31185126-aee4-5f3a-8c86-bf5d945cdbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}5)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525326} {:answer/id #uuid "4388d98d-7715-5139-9370-ebc7eda1d940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}5)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525376} {:answer/id #uuid "439b617d-3959-59b7-b388-1a3da51ea756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525377} {:answer/id #uuid "6d204723-ba3a-5806-946f-685b2f2df678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}5)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31185126-aee4-5f3a-8c86-bf5d945cdbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}5)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525326} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f436295-4cc3-5f5b-b35d-95e76cf32d28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "adbf3121-a1d5-5610-b22f-f0bc48a8db12" :question/math-academy-id "q-87487" :question/problem "The graph of $y=6f(4x-4)+5,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $6$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translate up by $5$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}5)$ - Translate right by $1$ unit\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=6f(4x-4)+5=6f(4(x-1))+5\n$$\n\nTo get $y=6f(4(x-1))+5,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(4x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}5).$ Translate $y=f(4x)$ right by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(4(x-1)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Stretch $y=f(4(x-1))$ by a scale factor of $6$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=6f(4(x-1)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=6f(4(x-1))$ up by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=6f(4(x-1))+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}5)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078512 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d69a3ef2-0854-503b-8586-b0113966d9ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Four Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ee6f530-d95e-57a0-af25-c2a599a4fca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/8576e182b199885a0c93efa40ed28cd50f20fee03fb3cb9094ddf9f636b28942.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525406} {:answer/id #uuid "54b9772e-234b-5fb8-925d-81356e243b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9fe0db9b8576acb8bd7f89222fc177d07e115cc4a52b1bb0d1ec406868ceaa20.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525427} {:answer/id #uuid "565f0b0d-5cbf-5a11-9d31-f10f9dc7380c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/da0530d2c06dd94716ea79bbfe2923fc5fad5a4d6a6da3dd435be84920f8a692.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525429} {:answer/id #uuid "ce56a25c-7a51-579f-8de2-52dda9ab57ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/300a6358abf734bc3960fe39e4c28fd9b1174697faa6f5db44fa2c516974d04d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525718} {:answer/id #uuid "d55e78a9-2a3d-5ee5-9e4e-cfda58276a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/227777072e65f93aaac50a82e63378663de1c71da4ffd1f8ea86869628d1da16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d55e78a9-2a3d-5ee5-9e4e-cfda58276a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/227777072e65f93aaac50a82e63378663de1c71da4ffd1f8ea86869628d1da16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525737} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb181ae6-2f97-5c23-bf32-d7b7ada1a42f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e7bd953-d64f-5fc1-9cc8-824abcf9bec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-87529" :question/problem "![](images/98/98ae486b18ff78601faf8a99f5310df982a73f0403ecf43186eef947b847bed4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1))-8?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1))-8,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}=4$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x).$\n\n![](images/b6/b6b847be6f77123d3baec5f3541caf2a151039e35a5aad68b0228508d84dbc88.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)).$\n\n![](images/8d/8da2b9a2d31c082c069a066709afd5346c702c75a96b840d1c6ee5d9f940bf8f.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)).$\n\n![](images/fd/fd1ee07f396bf7561dc93cf4eb5f4098bb0c465c6f4b4a83328751d7e9a65dfa.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $8$ units to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1))-8.$\n\n![](images/b9/b9f282fc14207257835dbf9ce368d07e68cb207a04875a64cccfe07f31ef5d32.png)"}]}
